---
description: Non-boolean column defaults belong on the model, not the migration
alwaysApply: true
---

# No non-boolean database defaults

Do not put a `default:` on a migration column unless the column is boolean. String, integer, enum, and timestamp defaults are hard to change later. Set them on the model.

```ruby
# bad
create_table :memberships do |t|
  t.string :role, null: false, default: "operator"
end
```

```ruby
# good
create_table :memberships do |t|
  t.string :role, null: false
end

class Membership < ApplicationRecord
  enum :role, { operator: "operator", admin: "admin" }, default: :operator, validate: true
end
```

Boolean columns may keep a database default (`default: false`).
